  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 336 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nAs mentioned above, an active weather pattern will continue into \nThursday. The upper low will continue to move eastward into South \nDakota, potentially putting the area into a dry slot by the \nafternoon. Nevertheless, there is increasing confidence that most \nlocations could see up to at least a half inch of rainfall, with \nsome locations along and north of I80 in the Sandhills having a 30 \nto 50 percent chance of receiving an inch total. The longer \nduration of this system should mostly preclude any potential \nflooding concerns, but will be watched closely, particularly \nalong the I80 region as the event takes shape. \n\nBy Thursday afternoon, the precipitation should begin to push out of \nthe area. Wanted to mention a possibility of some isolated fire\nweather concerns in northern Sheridan and northwestern Cherry \ncounties, as far south of McKelvie National Forest where \nhumidity could drop to 30 - 40 percent overlapped with 35 to 40 \nmph wind gusts. This will all be dependent on exactly where the\ndry slotting takes place as well as how long any precipitation \nlingers into the afternoon. \n\nAs the upper trough moves off to the east into the Midwest region, \nstrong upper ridging will return across the Pacific Northwest. With \nthis ridge, cold air advection will ride south with northerly flow \naloft out of Canada and push a much colder, drier airmass into the \nNorthern Plains. This airmass will arrive to our region late \nThursday night to early Friday morning. This will plunge low \ntemperatures into the low 30s across most of the CWA with the \neastern Panhandle potentially dropping to the upper 20s. While \nSaturday morning could see lows in the low to mid 30s across the \nregion. With these widespread low temperatures, confidence in \nFrost/Freeze headlines grows with each forecast cycle. Opted to wait \nfor this forecast cycle to see if the trend continues to hold but \nhave added frost Friday morning to highlight this concern. Stay \ntuned for the latest information on frost/freeze potential. \n\nSwitching gears to the weekend and early next week, the upper ridge \ncontinues to move across the Rockies and into the Plains by Saturday \nafternoon. this will bring daytime highs back into the upper 60s to \nlow 70s across western and north central Nebraska through the early \nnext week. Depending on how the precipitation system fairs from \nearlier in the week, will better determine the potential severity \nfor any fire weather concerns through the weekend. As it stands now, \n areas west of Hwy 83 is highlighted for the potential of some \nelevated fire weather concerns where locations could see humidity \ndrop as low as 25 percent Saturday afternoon. This will have some \noverlap with strong southerly wind gusts of 30 to 35 mph.",
